\begin_layout Standard
To simulate the exact polarizers from Stuart Freedman’s 1972 Ph.D.
 thesis (Experimental Test of Local Hidden-Variable Theories,
 UC Berkeley / Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory),
 you need to model his custom pile-of-plates polarizers using their experimentally measured principal transmittances rather than ideal polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
1.
 Freedman's 1972 Hardware & Optical Parameters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the 1972 calcium atomic cascade experiment,
 Freedman and Clauser needed large-aperture polarizers with high transmission,
 so they built two "pile-of-plates" polarizers,
 each consisting of ten 0.3 mm-thick glass sheets inclined near Brewster's angle (~56.3°).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because glass plates at Brewster's angle reflect some perpendicular (s-polarized) light at each surface while transmitting nearly all parallel (p-polarized) light,
 they act as imperfect linear polarizers with non-zero leakage and insertion loss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the Freedman & Clauser (1972) paper,
 the measured principal transmittances were:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Polarizer 1 (551.3 nm green photon 
\begin_inset Formula $γ₁$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Parallel transmittance:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{1}_{M}=0.97\pm0.01)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Perpendicular transmittance (leakage):
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{1}_{m}=0.038\pm0.004$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Polarizer 2 (422.7 nm violet photon 
\begin_inset Formula $γ₂$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Parallel transmittance:
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{2}_{M}=0.96\pm0.01$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Perpendicular transmittance (leakage):
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{2}_{m}=0.037\pm0.004$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Rotation Increments:
 Each polarizer was rotated via a Geneva mechanism in discrete steps of 22.5°.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Exact Jones Matrix for a Pile-of-Plates Polarizer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a polarizer oriented at angle 
\begin_inset Formula $α$
\end_inset

 with major transmittance 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{M}$
\end_inset

 and minor transmittance 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{m}$
\end_inset

 the amplitude transmission coefficients are 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\sqrt{\epsilon_{M}}}and\ensuremath{\sqrt{\epsilon_{m}}}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $P(\alpha,\epsilon_{M},\epsilon_{m})=\left(\begin{matrix}\sqrt{\epsilon_{M}}\cos^{2}\alpha+\sqrt{\epsilon_{m}}\sin^{2}\alpha & (\sqrt{\epsilon_{M}}-\sqrt{\epsilon_{m}})\cos\alpha\sin\alpha\\
(\sqrt{\epsilon_{M}}-\sqrt{\epsilon_{m}})\cos\alpha\sin\alpha & \sqrt{\epsilon_{M}}\sin^{2}\alpha+\sqrt{\epsilon_{m}}\cos^{2}\alpha
\end{matrix}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a photon in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 passes through the transmission probability is 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\langle\psi_{\text{out}}\vert{}\psi_{\text{out}}\rangle$
\end_inset

 that reproduces the exact experimental single-photon transmission formula from Freedman's thesis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
T(\theta) & = & \epsilon_{M}\cos^{2}(\theta-\alpha)+\epsilon_{m}\sin^{2}(\theta-\alpha)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(Note:
 Because the glass plates can be folded out of the optical path;
 a key feature Freedman used to normalize coincidence counts 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R(φ)}{R₀}$
\end_inset

 the Ada code  includes an In_Path toggle.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
2.
 Ada Implementation (freedman_1972_sim.adb)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This Ada program implements both Freedman Polarizer 1 and Polarizer 2,
 supports folding them in and out of the beam path and verifies both single-beam transmission and two-photon entangled singlet state correlations 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ensuremath{\vert\Psi\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\vert H_{1}H_{2}\rangle+\vert V_{1}V_{2}\rangle)})$
\end_inset

 across Freedman's 22.5° and 67.5° Bell-test angles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Expected Output & Bell Inequality Violation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Running this simulation exactly reproduces the curve from Figure 3 of Freedman's thesis (accounting for the ~3.8% leakage of the 10-plate glass stacks):
 
\end_layout

